---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import LegalIdentity from '../../components/LegalIdentity.astro';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { AUTHOR_NAME, AUTHOR_DESC, CONTACT_EMAIL, SITE_NAMES } from '../../data/site-config';
import { srcLink } from '../../lib/helpers';
import { KIT as K } from '../../lib/kit';
import { formatNumber } from '../../lib/format';
import { profilePageSchema, breadcrumbSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
const lang = 'en';
const path = route('about', lang);
const title = 'Bridge Days Germany 2027 publisher: who we are, sources';
const description = 'Who publishes the 2027 bridge-day planner: Radif Partners, independent and selling no travel. Where the holidays, municipality lists and Easter dates come from.';
const s = (k: string, t: string) => srcLink(k, lang, t);
const M = K.P.bayern_mh;
---
<PageLayout title={title} description={description} lang={lang} jsonLd={[profilePageSchema({ path, lang }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'About', path }])]} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'About' }]}>
  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <h1>About {SITE_NAMES[lang]}</h1>
    <p>{AUTHOR_DESC.en}</p>
    <h2>Why this site exists</h2>
    <p>When you book next year's leave in the autumn, you look for the days where one day off turns into a long weekend. Most lists of those days sit on pages that want to sell you a trip afterwards, show only nationwide dates, or forget that Augsburg has two more public holidays than Nuremberg. This site works through each of the sixteen German states separately, includes holidays that apply in only part of a state, and shows the best plan for exactly the number of leave days you want to spend.</p>
    <h2>Who publishes it</h2>
    <p>The publisher is {AUTHOR_NAME}, a company that builds tools from public rules and data. {AUTHOR_NAME} is not an authority or a law firm, has no link with any state government and uses no coats of arms or logos. The site sells no trips, hotels or calendars and carries no affiliate links.</p>
    <LegalIdentity lang={lang} />
    <h2>Where the data comes from</h2>
    <p>Public holidays come from the sixteen state holiday acts, such as the <Fragment set:html={s('ftg-by', 'Bavarian Public Holiday Act')} /> or the <Fragment set:html={s('ftg-nw', 'North Rhine-Westphalia act')} />; 3 October comes from the <Fragment set:html={s('einigungsvertrag', 'Unification Treaty')} />. Which {formatNumber(M.mit, 0, lang)} of the {formatNumber(M.gemeinden, 0, lang)} Bavarian municipalities have 15 August off was determined by the <Fragment set:html={s('by-mh', 'Bavarian State Office for Statistics')} />; you can search that list here. Easter is computed with the <Fragment set:html={s('ostern-ptb', 'Easter formula published by the PTB')} />, Germany's national metrology institute, and the clock changes follow the <Fragment set:html={s('sozv', 'Summer Time Ordinance')} />. How bridges are computed is on the <a href={route('method', lang)}>method</a> page; how we correct mistakes is in the <a href={route('editorial', lang)}>editorial policy</a>.</p>
    <h2>Your data</h2>
    <p>Every calculator runs in your browser. The state, leave days and municipality you enter are neither sent nor stored. There is no account and no form. Details are in the <a href={route('privacy', lang)}>privacy notice</a>.</p>
    <h2>What the site does not do</h2>
    <p>It does not approve leave and does not know your employment contract. Whether Christmas Eve and New Year's Eve are off for you, whether your employer closes on bridge days or whether a collective agreement gives more leave is in your own documents. School holidays are not covered.</p>
    <h2>Write to us</h2>
    <p><a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> if a holiday, a date or a municipality looks wrong.</p>
  </article>
</PageLayout>
